1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,560
Torn meniscus - if your knee buckles or gives way going down stairs, are you brushing it off?

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:11,920
Between your thighbone and shinbone sits a C-shaped cushion of cartilage called the meniscus, and a tear happens when that cartilage rips.

3
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:24,720
That buckling feeling on stairs comes from the joint losing stability, and you may also notice catching, clicking, or a knee that won't fully straighten - about 850,000 meniscus procedures happen in the US every year.

4
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:27,120
There are two very different causes.

5
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:34,960
In young, active people, it's usually a forceful twist with the foot planted on the ground, common in soccer, basketball, and football.

6
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:44,800
In older adults, the cartilage simply wears down over time, so a tear can happen with no injury at all, sometimes from something as small as stepping on uneven ground.

7
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:47,920
Ignoring a torn meniscus speeds up arthritis.

8
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:56,160
Once the cartilage can't cushion the joint anymore, bone grinds against bone, and that accelerates cartilage loss and knee osteoarthritis.

9
00:00:56,480 --> 00:01:02,000
First, rest the knee and ice it for 15 to 20 minutes every 3 to 4 hours for a few days.

10
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:06,080
Skip deep squats and twisting movements until the swelling calms down.

11
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:08,480
Second, build quad strength.

12
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:16,160
Sit with your leg straight, tighten your thigh muscle, and hold for 5 seconds - stronger quads take pressure off the joint itself.

13
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:23,840
See a doctor right away if your knee gets stuck bent or straight and you can't move it, if you can't bear weight, or if swelling gets worse fast.
